# Chamber mixer, printed monolithic — the case where drainage matters.
#
# Two streams meet, drop into a 4 mm spherical chamber that stalls and folds
# them, then run a short serpentine to the outlet. Printed as one solid block
# rather than a two-piece chip, so nothing is open until the resin is flushed:
# every enclosed cavity needs a path out, and the node furthest from any port
# is where a vent does the most good.
#
# `examples/chamber-mixer.openchip` is this exact file compiled — read the two
# side by side to see what each DSL line becomes in raw graph form.

chip "Chamber mixer"
printer high-res-8k
size 40 x 60
base 6
wall 2
edge-spacing 3
split monolithic
channel 0.8 x 0.5 rect

port inletA at 12,52 luer-slip inlet water 15
port inletB at 28,52 luer-slip inlet pbs 15
port outlet at 20,8  luer-slip outlet

node junction at 20,46
chamber pot at 20,40 sphere 4

path inletA -> junction
path inletB -> junction
path junction -> pot

node mid at 20,32
path pot -> mid -> (20,24) -> (20,16) -> outlet

# `mid` is 24 mm of channel from the nearest port — the local maximum, and so
# the one place resin has furthest to travel to escape.
vent mid 0.8
